  "sections": [
    {
      "key": "quickFacts",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/09-characoids-tetra-relationship/brittanichthys-axelrodi-2/",
      "html": "<p>These small treasures grow to a maximum size of 4 cm.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "distribution",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/09-characoids-tetra-relationship/brittanichthys-axelrodi-2/",
      "html": "<p>These interesting, rarely traded characins come from the catchment area of the Rio Negro.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "waterConditions",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/09-characoids-tetra-relationship/brittanichthys-axelrodi-2/",
      "html": "<p>Brittanichthys axelrodi is a true black water fish, which should be kept at pH 4.5 – 6 and low hardness.</p><p>Filtering via peat can be very helpful here. In addition to improving the chemical parameters, the bactericidal effect of peat also reduces the bacterial pressure in the aquarium.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "diet",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/09-characoids-tetra-relationship/brittanichthys-axelrodi-2/",
      "html": "<p>During the acclimatisation period, small live food such as Artemia nauplii or sieved pond food should be fed. Frozen food is also accepted, and a change to dry food can also be tried.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "dimorphism",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/09-characoids-tetra-relationship/brittanichthys-axelrodi-2/",
      "html": "<p>The males can be recognized by the strange glandular scales in the caudal fin, whose meaning is unknown.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "reproduction",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/09-characoids-tetra-relationship/brittanichthys-axelrodi-2/",
      "html": "<p>There are indications of internal fertilization: during scientific research sperm have been found in females.</p><p>Peter and Martin Hoffmann published a detailed breeding report in the German magazine DATZ (Issue 3/2010, page 18).</p>"
    }
  ],
